From: Anand Joseph <anajoseph@nvidia.com>
Subject: ggml-cuda: batch dimension for the fused NanoCodec convolutions

GGML_OP_CONV1D_FUSED (plain and grouped) took one sequence: x [T, cin, 1]
with its streaming cache [pad, cin, 1]. Accept a batch of independent
sequences, x [T, cin, B] with caches [pad, cin, B] (and residuals
[T, cout, B]), so a TTS server decodes the codec chunks of many concurrent
requests in one launch per convolution instead of one small launch each.

Time tiles of all sequences share grid.x. The split-K count is chosen from one
sequence's tiles, so every sequence sums its channel chunks exactly as a
batch-1 launch does and its output is bit-identical. The per-stream split-K
scratch now grows on demand (outside CUDA graph capture) instead of dropping
split-K when a launch needs more than the initial 4 MB.

ggml_conv_transpose_1d accepted only a matrix input although its CUDA
kernels already index every output by batch plane: accept [T, cin, B] there
too (the CPU kernel still takes one sequence and asserts it).

The half-snake fusion matched only unbatched activations, whose channel
slices are contiguous views. A batch's slices are strided and made
contiguous by CONT nodes; match that form too and read the leaky-ReLU slice
through its view (the kernel now takes strides), so a batched activation
runs the same fused arithmetic per element as an unbatched one.

Upstream: stays here (the ops are project-specific; the conv_transpose_1d
batch could go upstream with a CPU kernel).

